  {
   "id": "WJWMMcky02QJ",
   "from": "Anja",
   "author": "a9b1fc17ce131310",
   "date": "2015-07-08T22:57:24Z",
   "subject": "Dichotomous outcome varibles and missing data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">I&#39;m quite new user of lavaan package and I\r\nwould really appreciate some help.</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><br></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">My model<span> \r\n</span>has an endogenous latent variable composed of 5 dichotomous measured\r\nvariables. Therefore, I used parameter ‘orderd’ inside sem function as\r\npresented below. The model was well estimated on completely observed cases. <br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><br></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Now, I would like to use the whole dataset also\r\nwith missing values. I add the parameter missing=”fiml” inside the sem\r\nfunction. The warning occurs that “DWLS” estimator could not be used with\r\nincomplete data.</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Do you have any suggestions how to analyse my\r\nmodel with incomplete data?</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><br></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Thanks in advance, Anja <br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><br><span lang=\"EN-GB\"></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"></span></p><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">myModelSEM</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39; <br># latent variable definitions<br>MC =~ mc1+mc2+mc3+mc4 <br>PT =~ pt1+pt2+pt3 <br>EU =~ eu1+eu2+eu3+eu4 <br>PU =~ pu1+pu2+pu3+pu4+pu5+pu6+pu7 <br>BI=~ bi1+bi2+bi3+bi4+bi5   #dichotomous var.<br># regressions<br>EU ~ MC <br>EU~ PT <br>PU ~ MC <br>PU ~ PT <br>PU ~ EU <br>BI ~ EU <br>BI ~ PU <br>BI ~ Q5 <br>&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>semM</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">myModelSEM</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ordered</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi1&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi2&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi3 &quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">bi4</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;,&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">bi5</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;)) <br> summary(semM, fit.measures=TRUE, standardized=TRUE)</span><span style=\"color:#080\"><br></span></div></code></div><br><br><p></p></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "tttWD7vZX_IJ",
   "from": "yrosseel",
   "author": "89c9fc822e34b78c",
   "date": "2015-07-09T04:58:55Z",
   "subject": "Re: Dichotomous outcome varibles and missing data",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On 07/09/2015 12:57 AM, Anja wrote:\r<br>&gt; Do you have any suggestions how to analyse my model with incomplete data?\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "For categorical data, you can try missing = &quot;pairwise&quot;.\r<br>\r<br>Yves.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "GltDWBzPksMJ",
   "from": "Anja",
   "author": "a9b1fc17ce131310",
   "date": "2015-07-09T07:31:58Z",
   "subject": "Re: Dichotomous outcome varibles and missing data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Yves, thank you very much. The pairwise option is working fine (the path coefficents show even slightly stronger relationship and fit indices are better according to thresholds)..<br><br>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Would you\r\nsuggest in that type of model reporting DWLS indices or robust versions (or\r\nboth)?</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Since you\r\nan expert in SEM, can I kindly ask you for your (personal) opinion. Since\r\nreviewer explicitly suggested using ‘fiml’ method how to justify using ‘pairwise’?\r\n<br>\r\nOr it would be better to impute (only) once missing values with e.g. k-nearest\r\nneighbours algorithm and run sem on that imputed dataset?</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"><br></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Thank you\r\nin advance, Anja<span>  </span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<br><br>Dne četrtek, 09. julij 2015 06.58.55 UTC+2 je oseba yrosseel napisala:</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">On 07/09/2015 12:57 AM, Anja wrote:\r\n<br>&gt; Do you have any suggestions how to analyse my model with incomplete data?\r\n<br>\r\n<br>For categorical data, you can try missing = &quot;pairwise&quot;.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Yves.\r\n<br>\r\n<br></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Z1cG422nWtYJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2015-07-09T10:52:39Z",
   "subject": "Re: Dichotomous outcome varibles and missing data",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Would you\r\nsuggest in that type of model reporting DWLS indices or robust versions (or\r\nboth)?</span></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Robust shows better performance in simulation studies:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.2012.02049.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.2012.02049.x&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429758260000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3MUxxz2nn6CdMMvWfRYi04\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.2012.02049.x</a></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Since you\r\nan expert in SEM, can I kindly ask you for your (personal) opinion. Since\r\nreviewer explicitly suggested using ‘fiml’ method how to justify using ‘pairwise’? <br></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>It is a question of availability.  Maximum Likelihood estimation in SEM software packages is always maximum-multivariate-normal likelihood.  Binary data are not multivariate-normally distributed, so you cannot use maximum (normal) likelihood -- FIML or otherwise.</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1037/a0029315\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1037/a0029315&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429758260000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3dv-8MopObi4CgG0zo_V85\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1037/a0029315</a></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">\r\nOr it would be better to impute (only) once missing values with e.g. k-nearest\r\nneighbours algorithm and run sem on that imputed dataset?</span></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Why only 1 imputation, and why that particular algorithm?  You should use multiple imputation, which is a preferred modern method for dealing with missing data because it only assumes MAR data, whereas pairwise deletion assumes MCAR.  This article provides conceptual introductions to MAR and MCAR assumptions, as well as FIML and multiple imputation, and includes some R syntax for an applied example:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/jpepsy/jst048\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/jpepsy/jst048&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429758260000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2WniuvXeKwIEqwD6Z2t4SD\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/jpepsy/jst048</a></div><div><br></div><div>To use multiple imputation with lavaan, look into the R package &quot;semTools&quot; -- it includes a function<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> runMI() </font>that automates the process of (1) imputing missing data multiple times (using the &quot;mice&quot; or &quot;Amelia&quot; packages in R), (2) running your SEM on all imputed data sets using lavaan, and (3) combining the results.</div><div><br></div><div>Terry</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ru2-GVGOd0gJ",
   "from": "Anja",
   "author": "a9b1fc17ce131310",
   "date": "2015-07-13T20:35:39Z",
   "subject": "Re: Dichotomous outcome varibles and missing data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Terry, thank you very much. Your answers and papers clarify some issues :)<br><br>I try to use the runMI function, but unsuccessfully. Could you please have a look where I made a mistake. Probably it is something stupid, but I can&#39;t figured it out, what&#39;s wrong.<br><br>Thanks in advance, Anja<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#800\">## I&#39;m using the following packages</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">##  mice_2.22           lattice_0.20-29   semTools_0.4-6      lavaan_0.5-19.863 Amelia_1.7.3  </span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">## the following code is not working</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>dataMI </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> amelia</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">m</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ords</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">26</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\">    </span><span style=\"color:#800\">### ords indicates that I would like to have all variables to be ordered (1-5, and the last five variables should be dichotomous)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>modelMI</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> runMI</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">myModelSEM</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dataMI$imputations</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> fun</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;sem&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ordered</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi1&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi2&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi3&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi4&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi5&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Error</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">`[&lt;-`</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">`*tmp*`</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lhs</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rhs</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> value </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.0087718452194632</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>  subscript </span><span style=\"color:#008\">out</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> of bounds<br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> addition</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Warning</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> message</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">In</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lavData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">label </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">label</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  lavaan WARNING</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data argument has been coerced to a data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame<br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\">## this code is working well for all imputed datasets (not just for the first one)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>modelMI_imp1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">myModelSEM</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dataMI$imputations</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ordered</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi1&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi2&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi3&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi4&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bi5&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>summary</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">modelMI_imp1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">measures</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> standardized</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><br><br><br><br>Dne četrtek, 09. julij 2015 12.52.39 UTC+2 je oseba Terrence Jorgensen napisala:</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Would you\r\nsuggest in that type of model reporting DWLS indices or robust versions (or\r\nboth)?</span></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Robust shows better performance in simulation studies:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.2012.02049.x\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.2012.02049.x&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429758264000&amp;usg=AOvVaw19g3aMG2T-m_Llt0B2-HcJ\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.2012.02049.x</a></div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Since you\r\nan expert in SEM, can I kindly ask you for your (personal) opinion. Since\r\nreviewer explicitly suggested using ‘fiml’ method how to justify using ‘pairwise’? <br></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>It is a question of availability.  Maximum Likelihood estimation in SEM software packages is always maximum-multivariate-normal likelihood.  Binary data are not multivariate-normally distributed, so you cannot use maximum (normal) likelihood -- FIML or otherwise.</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1037/a0029315\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1037/a0029315&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429758264000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0w_8zdMSAPQjO8nzYdCJbu\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1037/a0029315</a></div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">\r\nOr it would be better to impute (only) once missing values with e.g. k-nearest\r\nneighbours algorithm and run sem on that imputed dataset?</span></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Why only 1 imputation, and why that particular algorithm?  You should use multiple imputation, which is a preferred modern method for dealing with missing data because it only assumes MAR data, whereas pairwise deletion assumes MCAR.  This article provides conceptual introductions to MAR and MCAR assumptions, as well as FIML and multiple imputation, and includes some R syntax for an applied example:</div><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/jpepsy/jst048\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/jpepsy/jst048&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429758264000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3PdM4n36md7N9XJ908QaYD\">http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/jpepsy/jst048</a></div><div><br></div><div>To use multiple imputation with lavaan, look into the R package &quot;semTools&quot; -- it includes a function<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> runMI() </font>that automates the process of (1) imputing missing data multiple times (using the &quot;mice&quot; or &quot;Amelia&quot; packages in R), (2) running your SEM on all imputed data sets using lavaan, and (3) combining the results.</div><div><br></div><div>Terry</div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
